{% comment %}The component panel and its filters swap together. Its modal remains outside this region.

It also swaps itself for a saved triage decision. Triage writes the VEX in the request but
re-annotates the stored scan results on the queue, so the decision reaches these rows in
two steps: the save dispatches refresh-assessments straight away, and
reapply_vex_to_component_scans broadcasts vex_reapplied when the re-annotation lands. Both
arrive here as the same event. Without this the modal would close on a toast and the row it
was opened from would keep its pre-triage state until the reader reloaded the page by hand.

The debounce matches the artifact page's: a workspace broadcast can arrive more than once
for one re-apply, and refetching the panel per message is wasted work.{% endcomment %}
<div id="component-vulnerabilities-table"
     x-data="{ refreshTimer: null }"
     hx-get="{{ vuln_panel_url }}?{{ vuln_refresh_query }}"
     hx-trigger="refresh-assessments from:body"
     hx-target="this"
     hx-swap="outerHTML"
     @ws:message.window=" if ($event.detail.type === 'vex_reapplied' && $event.detail.component_id === '{{ component_id }}') { clearTimeout(refreshTimer); refreshTimer = setTimeout(() => document.body.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('refresh-assessments')), 500); } ">
    <c-tables.shell nested>
    <c-vulnerabilities.filters :panel="vuln_panel" url="{{ vuln_panel_url }}" target="component-vulnerabilities-table" id="vuln" prefix="vuln_" push_url="true" />
    <c-vulnerabilities.table :rows="vuln_panel.rows" :can_triage="can_triage" />
    <c-vulnerabilities.footer :panel="vuln_panel" url="{{ vuln_panel_url }}" query="{{ vuln_query_string }}" prefix="vuln_" />
    </c-tables.shell>
</div>
